 ["What is the initial learning stage, in which a neutral stimulus is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus, called?","acquisition","extinction","generalization","During acquisition the association forms and the CR strengthens."],
 ["The bell is presented repeatedly without the food, and the salivation gradually fades. What is this called?","extinction","acquisition","spontaneous recovery","Extinction is the weakening of a CR when the CS is no longer paired with the US."],
 ["After a rest period, a dog salivates again to the bell even though extinction had occurred. What is this called?","spontaneous recovery","acquisition","discrimination","Spontaneous recovery is the reappearance of a weakened CR after a pause."],
 ["A dog conditioned to one tone also salivates to a similar tone. What is this called?","stimulus generalization","extinction","acquisition","Generalization is responding to stimuli similar to the CS."],
 ["Watson and Rayner\'s 1920 study of Little Albert is cited as showing which idea?","emotional responses can be learned through classical conditioning","fear is always innate","operant punishment removes fears","The study is cited for conditioned fear, but it raises serious ethical concerns and its methods and conclusions are criticized."],
 ["A person becomes sick hours after eating a certain food and later avoids that food. Which learning is this?","conditioned taste aversion","operant punishment","observational learning","Garcia and others showed that taste aversions can form after a single experience and a long delay."],
 ["A therapist has a client with a fear of dogs practice relaxation while gradually viewing dogs. Which classical conditioning technique is this?","counterconditioning","extinction of the US","spontaneous recovery","Counterconditioning replaces an unwanted response with an incompatible one, as in systematic desensitization."]
